-## 11. Security, Stealth & Policy
-
-### 11.1 Stealth Engine (`@sutradhar/browser/stealth/`)
-
-Layered anti-detection (ADR-0005):
-1. **Launch flags**: `--disable-blink-features=AutomationControlled`, `--no-sandbox`, `--disable-infobars`, window size, GPU flags.
-2. **Injected scripts** (`evaluateOnNewDocument`):
-   - `navigator.webdriver` → `undefined`.
-   - `window.chrome` mock (runtime/loadTimes/csi/app).
-   - WebGL vendor/renderer spoofed to NVIDIA RTX 3080.
-   - `navigator.hardwareConcurrency` (8), `platform` (Win32), `languages` (en-US, en).
-   - Default User-Agent: Chrome 122 on Windows 10.
+## 11. Security & Policy
+
+### 11.1 Detection-Evasion Boundary (no stealth engine)
+
+There is no stealth/anti-detection engine in this codebase — an earlier `@sutradhar/browser/stealth/`
+module (a dead, never-invoked `StealthEngine` plus 4 unused script generators) was removed
+outright as dead code, along with the no-op `enableStealth` option that never gated anything
+observable. Sutradhar launches without that detection-evasion machinery: it does not attempt to
+evade bot-detection or solve CAPTCHAs, and Cloudflare challenges, CAPTCHA walls, and IP-level
+blocks stop it exactly as they'd stop any other automation tool run the same way — with one
+narrow, honestly-stated exception, described next.
+
+`DEFAULT_LAUNCH_ARGS` unconditionally includes `--disable-blink-features=AutomationControlled`,
+kept for exactly one reason: it hides `navigator.webdriver` from simple client-side detection
+scripts (`navigator.webdriver` is `true` without it, `false` with it). It is not a stability
+flag — Chromium's own source (`bad_flags_prompt.cc`) lists `--disable-blink-features` among
+unsupported, developer-only flags, and a live headed launch with the flag present still shows
+Chrome's own "Chrome is being controlled by automated test software" banner. That webdriver hide
+is the full extent of it; it does not defeat any real bot-detection service, which relies on far
+more than that one signal, and the default headless user agent still contains the literal
+`HeadlessChrome` substring, which a simple check would also catch. See `AGENT_SETUP.md`'s "Known
+limitations" section for the same boundary stated for an operating agent.
 
